this is a production of Cornell  University  started them so I'm going to be talking  to you all today about a project I'm  working on trying to understand how  proteins adapt to high temperatures and  we know that temperature is important  and it affects both Mathieu macro  phenotypes like plant yield and  molecular characteristics of the cell so  for example we know that biochemistry  speeds up at high temperatures cell  membranes become more fluid and protein  structure and Composition adjust as well  because proteins need to maintain more  hydrogen bonds to maintain their  structure and this matters in the  context of plants and plant breeding  because the world is starting to warm up  and as different areas of the world  increase in temperature we're likely to  see a reduction in crop performance and  ideally what we could do is build  genomic selection models to help  breeders be selecting for crops that are  still efficient at these elevated  temperatures but from a protein  perspective this is fairly difficult  there's a number of roadblocks  preventing this us from doing this right  now  and probably the most significant  roadblock is just that we don't actually  understand right now which residues  matter within a protein and because in a  plant there could be thousands if not  millions of sites that actually matter  her protein stability and thermal  adaptation this becomes a fairly  daunting question to look at and that  leads to my research goals which are  actually to use a different system  in my case prokaryotes to be trying to  identify temperature sensitive sites  within the genome and really be  understanding the biochemistry behind  these sites to see what changes are  making proteins thermo tolerant and can  we rank or prioritize these temperature  sensitive sites and ultimately decide  what do we want to incorporate in these  future GS models now I'm doing this in  prokaryotes because they make a really  good system for studying molecular  adaptation there's a number of reasons  for that you can see six of them on the  screen here and I just want to highlight  two of them the first is this extreme  temperature box so we know that  prokaryotes can exist at  degrees Celsius and live and grow every  temperature through and even above 100  degrees Celsius so there's quite a bit  of quite a number of temperatures that  they're able to adapt to and they're  also useful because they tend to have  large effective population sizes and  fairly rapid generation time and that's  important because it means that  selection can act fairly efficiently on  every individual protein to adjust and  optimize its function at that prokaryote  optimal temperature now the other thing  I need to be able to look at protein  adaptation in prokaryotes is an  understanding of how prokaryotes are  growing and what their optimal growth  temperatures are so a lot of you  probably know em ricci men he is a  former postdoc in the buckler lab and he  and I built a model that uses tRNA  sequence from prokaryote genomes and  uses that to predict optimal growth  temperature or OGT and you can see from  this plot here on the left that this  works in both archaea and bacteria and  is fairly accurate and what's really  important about this model is that it  gives us the ability to predict optimal  growth temperature for any new  prokaryotes species we have and really  all we need is a genome to do that the  other thing that's really nice about  this model is that these predictions are  independent of protein composition and  other protein features so I can actually  use these optimal growth temperatures in  my questions looking at protein  adaptation so we're currently in the  process of writing up this paper but for  now I'm using this model to predict  optimal temperatures across almost 5,000  different prokaryotes species and so  that brings me to my current question  about protein adaptation and I have  three main hypotheses that I'm focused  on right now the first is fairly  straightforward  it's just that protein composition is  affected by temperature and I'm actually  not going to get into this in a lot of  detail this is fairly well documented  already and we know that there are some  amino acids that tend to shift and  become more prevalent at high  temperatures so I've checked this in the  data set that I'm working on and what I  see is fairly consistent with the  literature we tend to see more glutamic  acid  and baling and isoleucine and less  glycine so that lets me move on to my  second hypothesis which is that thermal  adaptation is highly polygenic so this  should seem fairly intuitive to you I've  already talked about some of the ways  the temperature affects cell function  and so if temperature is affecting every  protein we would expect to see changes  in just about every protein across the  genome and so to try and get an idea of  which proteins are important or which  residues within those proteins seem to  be adapting to temperature I've  developed a Jewess model to associate  specific protein domain residues with  optimal growth temperature of a  prokaryote and the way I do this is sort  of like this I take a set of species  proteomes from ensembl bacteria and that  includes both bacteria and archaea  species and I identify protein domains  using the pfm protein database and the  hidden Markov models in that database so  for example if I take this blue proteome  I can find three instances of protein  domain one and two instances of protein  domain two then I do a multiple sequence  alignment for all of those domain  observations and I numerically recode  that that protein domain just so that  it's easier to work with a linear  regression and then last I just  associate those residues with optimal  temperature now when I do this I get a  Manhattan plot so on the left here you  see an example Manhattan plot from one  protein domain the x-axis shows  positions in the protein domain  alignment the y-axis is our significance  and that red dotted line is the  bonferroni significance threshold and so  what might pop out to you here is that  there are a number of sites that show up  as significant in this example and  what's kind of fun about this is we can  actually look at the biochemistry and  understand what's going on at specific  sites so for example at this blue site  we see that we can look at amino acid  hydrophilicity and while it's not a  perfect correlation in general we see  that species with higher optimal  temperatures tend to have amino acids at  this site with low hydrophilicity where  species with moderate optimal  temperatures tend to have amino acids  with high hydrophilicity and this makes  a lot of sense and matches our  expectation because we already know that  proteins tend to be less hydrophilic at  high temperatures now when I do this  across all of the protein domains I'm  looking at I see that there are a lot of  domains with a lot of significant sites  essentially most of the domains I've  looked at have enrichments for  temperature associations about 90% of  the archaea domains and about 80% of the  bacteria and on average about a quarter  of each of these protein domains seems  to be a significantly associated with  temperature so that seems to confirm my  second hypothesis essentially thermal  adaptation does seem to be highly  polygenic and on average about a quarter  of the domain of every protein domain  seems to be associated with temperature  and so that brings me to my third  question or hypothesis which is that  genomes have a similar evolutionary  response to temperature and what i mean  by this is that there are likely to be  some key sites that transfer across  phylogenetic groups so I think that  there will be some sites that show up as  important in bacteria but also then end  up being important in archaea and these  sites are particularly interesting  because they're already conserved across  phylogenetic groups and that makes them  good candidates for us when we're  thinking about trying to extend this  model beyond prokaryotes and to  eukaryotes and so that's what I'm  working on right now is really trying to  identify these sites that are  significant across phylogenetic groups  and unfortunately it gets quite  complicated very quickly there are a lot  of sites that show up as significant and  unfortunately a lot of the sites showing  up as significant in archaea are also  significant in bacteria so I've just got  an example of two protein domains here  we have domain 4 and 118 and what I'm  showing you is a blue circle that shows  you how many sites are significant in  bacteria and a red circle that shows you  how many are significant in archaea now  when we overlap these we get that  pinkish purple color and you can see  that  focusing only on the site significant in  both of these phylogenetic groups does  reduce our search space but it still  leaves us with a lot of sites to be  looking at and so we need better ways to  prioritize these sites and unfortunately  the rank correlation between these  overlapping sites is fairly poor and so  we're not able to just prioritize these  sites based on their level of  significance so this third hypothesis is  very much still in progress it's true  that many sites appear significant  across phylogenetic groups but how we're  gonna prioritize these is still fairly  unclear and that brings me back  essentially to the beginning so I have  my research goal is to be identifying  these temperature sensitive protein  residues and I've told you a little bit  about how I've started to do that I'm  continuing now to try and reduce this  search space and predict which residues  are likely to matter and I really want  to be using the underlying biochemistry  to do this and ultimately as I've said  I'd like to be building models that can  transfer across phylogenetic groups  first across bacteria and archaea and  then ultimately to eukaryotes and  hopefully to plant proteins so we can  understand how plant proteins adapt to  temperature as well but that obviously  is not done yet and we'll be hopefully  the subject of a future talk but I'm  about out of time so I will wrap up  quickly say thank you to everybody in  the buckler lab for past and ongoing  conversations on this project and I'm  happy to take questions or comments if  you guys have them thank you Sara while  people are maybe taking some time to  write up some questions in the chat I'm  going to post the evaluation links here  ok and I see that John Luke has asked in  gos you have to control for structure  how do you do that in your adaptation  it's a really good question there's a  couple ways that I've tried to control  for structure so one of the ways is to  build a kinship matrix based on the  camer distance matrix from these species  genomes and another way that I've tried  to control for structure is using pcs on  the domains themselves to try and  account for some of the sequence  similarity within those domains  do either of those work well or change  the outcome  so the using the distance matrix based  on Kaymer frequency doesn't really seem  to change the outcome much using pcs  does seem to change it if I use  information about the sequence directly  does that answer your question I'm not  sure which Nikolas that is Rebecca asks  do I have plans to test kylo si  experimentally I do not any  collaborators in line for that there are  some people that work on directed  evolution in Chlamydomonas which would  be kind of cool but I haven't tried to  set anything up or really thought about  what that would look like John Lee I  think that temperature adaptation is  highly polygenic to answer your question  but I do still want to reduce the number  of low sigh that I'm looking at because  it it would be nice to prioritize the  sites in such a way that we can  um extend this more easily to eukaryotes  if that makes sense I think that there's  going to be a lot of sites changing in  bacteria and archaea because of  phylogeny or be cut for other reasons  that aren't necessarily going to be  interesting in eukaryotes and so  reducing the search space or at least  trying to understand some of the  biochemistry underlying the sites that  show up is significant I think will be  what's really important to extending the  model John Luke asked how many loci do I  ideally want to end up with that's a  good question and I I don't know right  now I'm focusing on transferability  between bacteria and archaea and I  haven't thought very much about sort of  a target number of low-side okay if  there are no further questions thank you  very much though thank you  this has been a production of Cornell  University on the web at Cornell edu

