ryotes and  hopefully to plant proteins so we can  understand how plant proteins adapt to  temperature as well but that obviously  is not done yet and we'll be hopefully  the subject of a future talk but I'm  about out of time so I will wrap up  quickly say thank you to everybody in  the buckler lab for past and ongoing  conversations on this project and I'm  happy to take questions or comments if  you guys have them thank you Sara while  people are maybe taking some time to  write up some questions in the chat I'm  going to post the evaluation links here  ok and I see that John Luke has asked in  gos you have to control for structure  how do you do that in your adaptation  it's a really good question there's a  couple ways that I've tried to control  for structure so one of the ways is to  build a kinship matrix based on the  camer distance matrix from these species  genomes and another way that I've tried  to control for structure is using pcs on  the domains themselves to try and  account for some of the sequence  similarity within those domains  do either of those work well or change  the outcome  so the using the distance matrix based  on Kaymer frequency doesn't really seem  to change the outcome much using pcs  does seem to change it if I use  information about the sequence directly  does that answer your question I'm not  sure which Nikolas that is Rebecca asks  do I have plans to test kylo si  experimentally I do not any  collaborators in line for that there are  some people that work on directed  evolution in Chlamydomonas which would  be kind of cool but I haven't tried to  set anything up or really thought about  what that would look like John Lee I  think that temperature adaptation is  highly polygenic to answer your question  but I do still want to reduce the number  of low sigh that I'm looking at because  it it would be nice to prioritize the  sites in such a way that we can  um extend this more easily to eukaryotes  if that makes sense I think that there's  going to be a lot of sites changing in  bacteria and archaea because of  phylogeny or be cut for other reasons  that aren't necessarily going to be  interesting in eukaryotes and so  reducing the search space or at least  trying to understand some of the  biochemistry underlying the sites that  show up is significant I think will be  what's really important to extending the  model John Luke asked how many loci do I  ideally want to end up with that's a  good question and I I don't know right  now I'm focusing on transferability  between bacteria and archaea and I  haven't thought very much about sort of  a target number of low-side okay if  there are no further questions thank you  very much though thank you  this has been a production of Cornell  University on the web at Cornell edu


